          <a href="#choosing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Choosing the Right Product</a>
          <a href="#tools" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. What You Need</a>
          <a href="#the-steps" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. The Steps</a>
          <a href="#tooling" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Tooling a Professional Bead</a>
          <a href="#weather" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Weather and Timing</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          A window is not supposed to be hermetically sealed. It is supposed to shed water down and out, and the caulking around it exists to direct that water rather than to trap it. Understanding that changes which lines you run a bead along.
        </p>

        <h2 id="where-to-seal" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. Where to Seal, and Where Not To
        </h2>

        <p>
          Get this wrong and the best sealant in the world makes the situation worse. Modern walls are drainage planes: water that gets past the outer skin is meant to run down behind it and escape at the bottom. Sealing the escape route turns a wall that sheds water into a wall that stores it.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Seal These</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Both vertical sides</strong> where the window frame or its trim meets the siding, brick or render. <strong>Across the top</strong>, where water running down the wall would otherwise reach the head of the window — unless there is a head flashing or drip cap, in which case seal to it rather than over it. <strong>Between the frame and the trim</strong> where a visible gap exists. <strong>Around any penetration</strong> nearby, such as a cable or vent passing through the wall.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Leave These Open</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>The underside of the sill.</strong> That gap is a drainage path. Water that gets into the assembly — and some always does — runs down and out along it. Seal the bottom and you have built a bathtub inside the wall.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Weep holes.</strong> The small slots along the bottom edge of vinyl and aluminium frames, and the open head joints at the base of brick veneer, are drainage by design. They look like defects and they are not.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Under a head flashing.</strong> Where a drip cap sits above the window, the gap beneath its leading edge lets water escape. Sealing it forces water back into the wall.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is the single most consequential part of the job, and it is covered in more depth in our guide to <a href="/where-not-to-caulk/">where not to caulk</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          2. Choosing the Right Product
        </h2>

        <p>
          Window joints move — the frame, the wall and the trim all expand at different rates, and a south-facing elevation swings through a wide temperature range every day. That rules out painter's caulk regardless of how tidy it looks going on.
        </p>

        <p>
          Buy a sealant rated for <strong>at least ±25% joint movement</strong>. If the joint will be painted as part of the trim scheme, that means a hybrid polymer or polyurethane. If the bead stays bare, silicone or a hybrid is right — silicone for the best UV life, hybrid if you want the option to paint later.
        </p>

        <p>
          Colour matters more than people expect on a bare bead: an exposed sealant line in the wrong shade draws the eye to every window on the elevation. Most manufacturers offer white, almond, grey, bronze and clear, and matching the trim rather than the siding usually looks better. Our <a href="/best-exterior-caulk/">exterior caulk reviews</a> cover which products held up in testing, and <a href="/caulk-vs-sealant/">caulk vs sealant</a> explains the movement rating in more detail.
        </p>

        <h2 id="tools" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. What You Need
        </h2>

        <p>
          A <strong>dripless caulk gun</strong> with a decent thrust ratio — the cheap ratchet guns make a neat bead considerably harder, and the difference costs very little. A <strong>utility knife</strong> and an <strong>oscillating tool or caulk removal blade</strong> for stripping the old bead. <strong>Backer rod</strong> in a size slightly larger than the gap. A <strong>caulk finishing tool</strong> or simply a gloved finger. <strong>Mineral spirits</strong> and rags if you are using silicone or polyurethane, or water and a cloth for acrylics and most hybrids. <strong>Painter's tape</strong> if the bead is visible and you want crisp edges.
        </p>

        <p>
          Add safe access. Upper-storey windows mean a stable ladder or scaffold, and caulking one-handed while holding onto a ladder is how beads end up messy and people end up hurt. If the windows are above single-storey height, this becomes a job worth planning properly rather than improvising.
        </p>

        <h2 id="the-steps" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. The Steps
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 1: Remove the Old Bead</h3>
        <p>
          Cut along both edges with a utility knife and pull the old sealant out in one length where you can. Scrape the residue with a putty knife or a dedicated removal blade. If the old material was silicone, remove every trace — nothing bonds to it, including new silicone. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-remove-old-caulk/">removing old caulk</a> covers the stubborn cases.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 2: Clean and Dry the Joint</h3>
        <p>
          Brush out dust and debris, then wipe the joint faces with a rag and denatured alcohol or mineral spirits, depending on the substrate. Let it dry completely. Sealant bonds to what it touches, so a joint that is dusty or damp will fail no matter how good the product is.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 3: Repair Before You Seal</h3>
        <p>
          Deal with rot, flaking paint and loose trim now. Sealant applied over failing paint is only as secure as that paint, and a bead over soft timber hides a problem that keeps growing. Scrape back to a sound edge, treat or replace bad wood, and prime bare timber before the bead goes on.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 4: Fit Backer Rod in Deep Gaps</h3>
        <p>
          For anything deeper than about a quarter inch, push closed-cell backer rod into the joint with a blunt tool until it sits at a depth that leaves the bead roughly half as deep as it is wide. Choose rod slightly wider than the gap so it stays put under compression. This step does two jobs: it stops you wasting sealant filling a cavity, and it prevents the bead bonding to the back of the joint.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 5: Cut the Nozzle to Match the Joint</h3>
        <p>
          Cut at roughly 45 degrees, starting small — a nozzle opening slightly narrower than the gap. You can always enlarge it; you cannot make it smaller. Puncture the inner seal, load the gun, and run a test bead on cardboard to check the flow before touching the window.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 6: Run the Bead</h3>
        <p>
          Hold the gun at about 45 degrees and <strong>push</strong> the bead ahead of the nozzle rather than pulling it behind. Pushing forces sealant into the joint; pulling drags it across the surface and leaves a hollow underneath. Move steadily and continuously, keeping the nozzle in contact with both faces, and work in runs you can complete without stopping — a restart mid-length always shows.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 7: Tool It Immediately</h3>
        <p>
          Within a couple of minutes, before any skin forms. One firm pass with a finishing tool or a gloved finger, applying enough pressure to push the sealant into the joint and leave a slightly concave surface. One pass only — going back over a tooled bead drags it and leaves the ridged look that gives amateur work away.
        </p>

        <h2 id="tooling" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. Tooling a Professional Bead
        </h2>

        <p>
          The difference between a bead that looks like a contractor did it and one that clearly did not comes down to three habits.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Concave, not convex.</strong> A properly tooled bead curves slightly inward. That shape is thinner in the middle and thicker where it meets each face, which is exactly how it should be to stretch without tearing. A domed bead sitting proud of the joint has the geometry backwards.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Consistent width.</strong> Nothing gives a job away faster than a bead that swells and narrows along its length. Consistency comes from a steady hand speed and even trigger pressure, which is far easier with a decent gun and a nozzle cut to the right size.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Tape for visible beads.</strong> On a prominent elevation, run painter's tape along both sides of the joint, apply and tool the bead, then pull the tape immediately while the sealant is still wet — pulling away from the bead at a shallow angle. It costs a few minutes and produces edges that look machine-cut.
        </p>

        <p>
          Work one window at a time from top to bottom, tooling as you go. Trying to run beads around three windows and then tool them all is how a whole elevation skins over before you get back to it.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How Much Sealant Will You Actually Need?</h3>

        <p>
          A standard 10.1 oz cartridge holds about 12 fluid ounces of material, which sounds abstract until you convert it to bead length. At a 1/4-inch bead you get roughly 55 linear feet from a tube. At 3/8 inch that drops to about 24 feet, and at a 1/2-inch bead you are down to somewhere near 14 feet. Those numbers assume a clean, consistent triangle and no waste, so treat them as a ceiling rather than a promise.
        </p>

        <p>
          Measure the perimeter of each opening you plan to seal and add the two vertical legs plus the head — remember you are deliberately leaving the sill underside open. A typical 3-by-5 double-hung window works out to roughly 13 linear feet of joint. That means one cartridge covers about four windows at a 1/4-inch bead, or barely one window if the gaps have opened up to half an inch. Buy one more tube than the arithmetic suggests. Running out mid-elevation forces you to either leave a joint open overnight or splice a cold joint into a bead that should have been continuous, and neither is a good outcome.
        </p>

        <h2 id="weather" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. Weather and Timing
        </h2>

        <p>
          Most sealants want the surface above roughly <strong>40°F</strong>, dry, and with no rain or frost during the cure window. Read the tube: silicone tolerates cold application better than most, while some acrylics and hybrids have a higher minimum.
        </p>

        <p>
          Avoid caulking in strong direct sun. A hot surface skins the bead within seconds, so it cannot be tooled properly, and applying to a joint at its maximum thermal expansion means the bead is stretched from the moment it cures. Working on the shaded side of the house and following the shade around the building is a genuinely useful habit.
        </p>

        <p>
          Late spring and early autumn are the ideal windows in most climates — mild, settled, and with the joint sitting somewhere near the middle of its annual movement range. That last point matters more than it sounds: a bead installed in a joint at its widest has only compression to handle, while one installed mid-range shares the movement in both directions.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Looking After the Beads Afterwards</h3>

        <p>
          Exterior window sealant is not a permanent installation, and treating it as one is why so many houses have joints that failed years before anyone noticed. Walk the elevations once a year, ideally in early spring when winter movement has done its worst. You are looking for three things: hairline separation along one face of the bead, which means adhesion is going; small transverse splits across the bead, which usually means the joint was over-filled or three-sided; and chalking or crumbling, which means the material has simply reached the end of its service life.
        </p>

        <p>
          Spot repairs work only if the surrounding bead is still sound and properly bonded. Cut back to material that will not lift when you pull at it, clean the substrate, and lap the new sealant onto the old by an inch at each end. Where more than about a quarter of a run has failed, strip the whole joint and start again — patching a failing bead just buys you one more season and leaves you doing the job twice.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing the Bottom of the Sill</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The most damaging mistake on this page. That gap drains the assembly; sealing it traps water inside the wall, where it rots framing invisibly for years. Sides and top only.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Caulking Over a Failing Bead</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                New sealant over old cracked material is only as strong as what is underneath. Cut it out, scrape the residue, and start from clean substrate — particularly with silicone, which nothing bonds to.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling a Deep Gap With Sealant Alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A deep bead cannot stretch and bonds to the back of the joint, so it tears at the first movement. Backer rod costs very little and is the difference between a five-year bead and a twenty-year one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pulling the Bead Instead of Pushing It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pulling lays sealant across the surface with a void behind it. Pushing forces it into the joint where it belongs. It feels less natural for about thirty seconds and then becomes automatic.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Painter's Caulk Outdoors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It tools beautifully and then splits along one edge within a season or two, because it has almost no movement capability. Exterior window joints need a rated sealant, not a filler.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Where should you caulk around exterior windows?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Both sides and across the top, where the frame or trim meets the siding or masonry. Leave the underside of the sill and any weep holes open — those are drainage paths, and sealing them traps water inside the wall.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What kind of caulk should I use?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">A sealant rated for at least ±25% movement. Hybrid polymer or polyurethane if the joint gets painted; silicone or a hybrid if it stays bare. Painter's acrylic latex is not adequate on an exterior window joint.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you have to remove the old caulk?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">If it is cracked, loose or silicone, yes — nothing bonds to a failing bead or to cured silicone. A sound, well-adhered acrylic bead can be cleaned and caulked over with a compatible product, though full replacement always performs better.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">When do you need backer rod?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">In anything deeper than about a quarter inch. It sets the bead depth to roughly half its width so it can stretch, and prevents three-sided adhesion — the bead bonding to the back of the joint, which is what tears beads apart.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What temperature can you caulk at?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Usually above about 40°F on a dry surface, with no rain or frost during the cure. Avoid strong direct sun too — a hot surface skins the bead before you can tool it, and the joint is at its widest.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it last?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">A quality sealant in a properly formed joint gives 15 to 25 years; acrylic latex on the same joint often fails in two to five. Early failure of a good product is nearly always geometry or preparation rather than the sealant itself.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Frame to siding, or just the trim?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Seal where trim meets siding and where frame meets trim, on the sides and top. What matters is that water running down the wall cannot get behind the assembly, and that anything which does get in can drain out at the bottom.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you caulk over paint?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Yes if the paint is sound and well adhered. If it is flaking, the sealant is only as secure as the paint under it — scrape back to a firm edge first, and prime any bare timber before applying the bead.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

