        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Seal the <strong class="text-white">sides and the top</strong>, leave the <strong class="text-white">bottom of the sill and the weep holes open</strong>, and use a sealant rated for at least ±25% movement rather than painter's caulk. Those three decisions matter more than any technique on this page.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            Then get the geometry right: old bead out, joint clean and dry, backer rod in anything deep, a bead about twice as wide as it is deep, pushed rather than pulled, and tooled concave in a single pass. Done that way on a mild dry day, the job takes an afternoon and lasts twenty years.
          </p>
